{
  "behaviorChanges": [
    "`tautline github-budget-status --identity` prints the GitHub identity this lane holds -- login, host, token source, and an eight-hex fingerprint that is never the token -- and REGISTERS this lane in the machine-local identity record. When two lanes on ONE MACHINE are active on the SAME upstream under ONE login, the surface warns and names the remedy (a per-lane GH_TOKEN bot PAT or a GitHub App installation token). Passing the flag is the act that declares this lane; a bare `github-budget-status` reads and never registers, so a passive status call cannot manufacture the collision it reports.",
    "A lane is a WORKTREE ROOT. An invocation from a subdirectory resolves to the lane its root owns, while sibling linked worktrees stay distinct lanes -- in this framework's standard one-worktree-per-lane topology they are exactly the concurrent lanes the advisory exists to catch, and collapsing them onto their shared git common dir made the warning unreachable. A checkout with no parseable remote never participates.",
    "Cross-machine sharing is NOT detected, and the canonical rule says so. The records are machine-local; two people on one login from two laptops is the same failure and this control is silent about it.",
    "The subsystem is advisory and cannot fail a lane: every failure path prints one `github_identity: unavailable - <reason>` line and leaves the exit code untouched. No network, no `gh`, and no credentials are all supported states. `MINERVIT_GITHUB_IDENTITY=0` stands it down entirely.",
    "`gh auth token` no longer takes the machine-wide gh serialization lock. It reads the local credential store and issues no API request, so serializing it bought zero rate-limit protection while costing up to the full 30s acquire timeout."
  ],
